e  shot at full speed you see how it's all  wiggly and i've undistorted the gopro  footage using optics compensation so i  take this shot and i bring it into boozu  and i just do a simple feature track and  then camera solve we actually have a  great booster tutorial that ren helped  us make if you guys want to see a little  bit more about how to track with buju  did a feature track which tracked all  the points in the image and then i did a  camera solve which then went on and  basically reconstructed where the camera  was in 3d space works great so then what  i do is i export the camera back into  after effects and we can see here a  couple of points i exported from buju  and we see they're tracking just fine to  the scene so now i'm going to do a handy  little trick generally known as  projection mapping or camera mapping red  actually has a great tutorial that he  did on how to do this so you have your  camera and you put a light right exactly  on the camera you can actually hold  shift and pick whip the light to the  camera from boujou and it'll put the  light exactly on the camera and parent  it to the camera and then i take the  footage layer and i duplicate it i make  it 3d i move it so it's just slightly  off the front of the camera and i shrink  it down so it's actually acting just  like a piece of film would in a camera  projector so now what i need to do is i  need to create a plane at the distance  of those points basically the ground  plane i then need to project the image  back onto that ground plane so here's  how i go about doing that in after  effects so i'm going to create a solid  and put line it up with that ground  plane you can go to layer new solid turn  that on i've already lined it up right  now there's not much to look at if i  turn off my background video layer  you're just gonna see a bunch of white  and a bunch of points and that's it so  now i need to project the image from the  camera so i named the camera coming out  of boujou the projector to make it  project next thing i will need to do is  duplicate my video layer and make it 3d  and then you move it exactly to the  camera and to do that i'll just hold  shift click on the pick whip and then go  over and select the projector so now the  footage plane has been lined up exactly  to the center of the camera right here  now it's obviously very big and if you  imagine film being going through  projector the film's actually really  small and just in front of the camera so  i'm going to do just that i'm going to  move the image just a little bit in  front of the camera so maybe one percent  size yeah a little small two percent  that'll do basically you wanted it to be  exactly lined up with the edges of your  frame oh and work on frame one next  thing you need to do is create a light  so you imagine a film projector you have  a light that is shining through that  film and casting a shadow onto a cinema  background cinema backing cinema drop  screen cinema screen layer new light  needs cast shadows that's technically  what happens when you shine light  through projector now this light once  again you move it to be exactly where  the camera is to turn that camera into  projector so hold shift click on the  pick whip and let go on projector so not  only doesn't move the light to be  exactly lined up with the camera we look  right here it also makes the light stick  to the camera so as the camera moves  through 3d space the light will stick  right with it projecting from the camera  so now here's where things get a little  fancy so i needed to tell after effects  this light needs to catch shadows i need  to tell after effects the white solid  that i have on my ground plane is only  going to receive shadows and i need to  tell after effects that that video layer  that i have scooted just in front of the  camera basically acting as our film that  we're going to project through and you  tell it that that is going to only cast  shadows and that's going to pass 100 of  the light through it here's my little  frame of film in front of the camera in  front of the light right here hit the  little drop down here go to material  options cast shadows set to only light  transmission 100 except lights can be  turned off so it's only catching shadows  and it's passing through 100 of the  light boom next up are white solid for  our settings for that we're going to go  down to our material options go to cache  shadows turn that off we only need it to  accept shadows and then our light of  course needs to have shadows turned on  so this is basically it here we have our  camera here we have our light and here  we have our little piece of the video  that's going to be projecting onto the  background down here on the ground we  have a white solid boom so now if we  watch this hopefully you can see how  this is working we see our 3d tracked  camera reprojecting the image that we  filmed with that camera onto a ground  plane that is lined up with the ground  plane that we 3d tracked you effectively  get a stabilized image it's all a little  crazy it's all a little weird it's a  little hard to describe but hopefully  with the visual of seeing how this  functions you can kind of wrap your head  around the concept anyways guys this is  how you 3d track a camera reproject the  footage in after effects and get rock  solid stabilizations using this  technique i hope you find it useful i  also want to give a big shout out to our  patrons who helped make this video  possible if you're not a patron you're  missing out on some cool tutorials for  example we're doing an interactive  tutorial on how to do this kind of  stabilization work  i will be answering questions and going  more in depth in the technique and how  it can be used in a lot of different  situations if you're watching this it's  already too late the video pledges have  already gone out but sign up for the  next one we do a lot of interesting  lessons and a lot of cool tutorials and